SUMMARY:[混合] Geophysics Master's Presentation\, Hamad Alswaidan: "Machi
 ne Learning-Assisted Full Waveform Inversion: Low-Frequency Extrapolation 
 and Cycle-Skipping Mitigation"
LOCATION:Mitchell Earth Sciences\, 350/372
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/geophysics-masters-presentation-hamad
 -alswaidan-machine-learning-assisted-full-waveform-inversion-low-frequency
 -extrapolation-and-cycle-skipping-mitigation
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/geophysics-masters-presentati
 on-hamad-alswaidan-machine-learning-assisted-full-waveform-inversion-low-f
 requency-extrapolation-and-cycle-skipping-mitigation\n線上資訊:\nhttps
 ://stanford.zoom.us/j/93984894466?pwd=2VqzQGageoyJcqQOp7vzVWzXr7DojF.1\n\n
 Full waveform inversion (FWI) is a powerful technique for estimating subsu
 rface physical properties from seismic data. However\, its nonlinear natur
 e and the limited availability of low-frequency seismic data make it susce
 ptible to cycle skipping\, often preventing convergence toward an accurate
  subsurface model.\n\nIn this work\, Hamad explores two complementary mach
 ine learning approaches to address these challenges. The first investigate
 s the use of generative diffusion models to extrapolate missing low-freque
 ncy content in seismic data. The second introduces a neural network-based 
 approach to mitigate cycle skipping through the alignment of observed and 
 modeled seismic waveforms during inversion.\n\nTogether\, these approaches
  explore how machine learning can complement physics-based inversion and i
 mprove the robustness of FWI.
